WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.566
Welcome to this Java tutorial, where you'll

00:00:03.566 --> 00:00:06.382
trace a for loop's starting step, test, and

00:00:06.382 --> 00:00:09.263
update, use an accumulator to keep a running

00:00:09.263 --> 00:00:12.079
total, and combine nested loops, one inside

00:00:12.079 --> 00:00:14.567
another, to visit every required pair.

00:00:15.301 --> 00:00:18.319
These skills help programs process a known

00:00:18.319 --> 00:00:21.552
number of items, check totals across repeated

00:00:21.552 --> 00:00:24.786
work, and cover combinations such as rows and

00:00:24.786 --> 00:00:26.151
columns in a table.

00:00:26.831 --> 00:00:30.117
A workshop assistant needs numbered labels for

00:00:30.117 --> 00:00:32.332
supply kits assigned to groups.

00:00:32.332 --> 00:00:35.619
Each label must identify one group and one kit

00:00:35.619 --> 00:00:36.905
within that group.

00:00:36.905 --> 00:00:40.262
Group numbers and kit numbers start at one, and

00:00:40.262 --> 00:00:43.406
every required pair needs exactly one label.

00:00:44.073 --> 00:00:46.926
Our report should list the kits for each group

00:00:46.926 --> 00:00:49.406
in order, then show the number of labels

00:00:49.406 --> 00:00:50.461
actually printed.

00:00:50.461 --> 00:00:53.252
We'll begin with a small practice batch, work

00:00:53.252 --> 00:00:55.980
through several groups, and then predict the

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:58.523
report for two groups with two kits each.

00:00:58.564 --> 00:01:01.692
Let's open Main.java in the Workspace and start

00:01:01.692 --> 00:01:03.289
with the practice batch.

00:01:09.075 --> 00:01:12.212
Main is the class containing our program, and

00:01:12.212 --> 00:01:15.349
its main method is where this program begins.

00:01:15.349 --> 00:01:18.694
We'll place our label-printing statements inside

00:01:18.694 --> 00:01:20.925
that familiar starter structure.

00:01:26.442 --> 00:01:29.529
Before printing any labels, we need a count of

00:01:29.529 --> 00:01:30.804
the work completed.

00:01:30.804 --> 00:01:33.085
We'll start labelsPrinted at zero.

00:01:33.085 --> 00:01:36.239
This variable will be our accumulator: it keeps

00:01:36.239 --> 00:01:39.192
a running result as the loop does more work.

00:01:41.944 --> 00:01:43.198
The count is ready.

00:01:43.198 --> 00:01:46.365
Our practice batch needs kits one through three,

00:01:46.365 --> 00:01:49.269
so a for loop can collect the starting step,

00:01:49.269 --> 00:01:51.645
condition, and update in one header.

00:01:51.645 --> 00:01:54.548
We'll start kit at one, allow values through

00:01:54.548 --> 00:01:57.254
three, and increase kit by one after each

00:01:57.254 --> 00:01:58.244
completed body.

00:02:03.179 --> 00:02:06.048
The header is in place, but its written order

00:02:06.048 --> 00:02:08.024
isn't the full execution order.

00:02:08.024 --> 00:02:09.809
Initialization happens once.

00:02:09.809 --> 00:02:12.614
Java tests the condition, runs the body only

00:02:12.614 --> 00:02:15.483
when that test is true, and then performs the

00:02:15.483 --> 00:02:17.268
update before testing again.

00:02:17.268 --> 00:02:20.264
We still need the body to print the current kit

00:02:20.264 --> 00:02:22.304
and record that completed print.

00:02:30.413 --> 00:02:33.152
Each body execution prints one label before

00:02:33.152 --> 00:02:34.489
increasing the count.

00:02:34.489 --> 00:02:37.037
Kit tells us which label we're handling;

00:02:37.037 --> 00:02:39.775
labelsPrinted tells us how many prints have

00:02:39.775 --> 00:02:40.348
finished.

00:02:40.348 --> 00:02:43.087
We initialized the count before the loop so

00:02:43.087 --> 00:02:44.743
earlier progress survives.

00:02:44.743 --> 00:02:47.736
After the loop stops, we'll print that retained

00:02:47.736 --> 00:02:49.838
count once and close the program.

00:02:56.104 --> 00:02:59.406
The summary sits after the loop's closing brace,

00:02:59.406 --> 00:03:01.745
so it won't repeat with every kit.

00:03:01.745 --> 00:03:04.772
Let's click Run to see the complete practice

00:03:04.772 --> 00:03:05.254
report.

00:03:10.424 --> 00:03:13.219
The report lists kits one, two, and three,

00:03:13.219 --> 00:03:15.149
followed by a count of three.

00:03:15.149 --> 00:03:18.210
After printing kit three, the update makes kit

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:18.542
four.

00:03:18.542 --> 00:03:21.670
Four fails the condition, so there is no fourth

00:03:21.670 --> 00:03:24.066
label and no further body execution.

00:03:24.066 --> 00:03:27.193
The count stays three, and the summary reads it

00:03:27.193 --> 00:03:28.524
after the loop ends.

00:03:28.624 --> 00:03:31.323
We can now print one numbered batch reliably.

00:03:31.323 --> 00:03:34.081
The workshop also needs to distinguish groups,

00:03:34.081 --> 00:03:36.900
so we'll replace the practice statements with a

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:39.538
report for three groups, each with two kits.

00:03:39.538 --> 00:03:42.417
A nested loop lets the outer loop choose a group

00:03:42.417 --> 00:03:45.175
while the inner loop visits that group's kits.

00:03:45.175 --> 00:03:47.574
We'll keep one count outside both loops.

00:03:55.916 --> 00:03:58.794
The outer loop starts with group one, and the

00:03:58.794 --> 00:04:01.225
count starts with no completed labels.

00:04:01.225 --> 00:04:04.104
Each time this outer body runs, it must visit

00:04:04.104 --> 00:04:06.470
that group's kits from the beginning.

00:04:06.470 --> 00:04:08.901
We'll put a second for loop inside it,

00:04:08.901 --> 00:04:11.523
initializing kit to one and allowing kits

00:04:11.523 --> 00:04:12.291
through two.

00:04:17.117 --> 00:04:19.981
The inner loop is now inside the group loop.

00:04:19.981 --> 00:04:22.911
For group one, it will visit kit one and then

00:04:22.911 --> 00:04:25.059
kit two before the group changes.

00:04:25.059 --> 00:04:28.119
To identify each pair, we'll build a label from

00:04:28.119 --> 00:04:30.983
the current group and kit, then increase the

00:04:30.983 --> 00:04:33.392
shared count after that label prints.

00:04:44.927 --> 00:04:47.804
The print uses both current values, and the

00:04:47.804 --> 00:04:50.011
count records one completed pair.

00:04:50.011 --> 00:04:53.088
We'll close the inner body first and the outer

00:04:53.088 --> 00:04:56.032
body second, then put the summary after both

00:04:56.032 --> 00:04:56.433
loops.

00:04:56.433 --> 00:04:59.377
That position makes it a report total rather

00:04:59.377 --> 00:05:01.852
than a separate total for each group.

00:05:09.661 --> 00:05:11.487
The nested report is complete.

00:05:11.487 --> 00:05:14.348
The kit name is available only within the inner

00:05:14.348 --> 00:05:17.208
for statement; code after that statement cannot

00:05:17.208 --> 00:05:17.635
use it.

00:05:17.635 --> 00:05:20.191
Each new outer iteration reaches the inner

00:05:20.191 --> 00:05:22.626
declaration again and starts kit at one.

00:05:22.626 --> 00:05:24.999
The same labelsPrinted variable remains

00:05:24.999 --> 00:05:27.860
available across both loops because we declared

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:28.773
it before them.

00:05:28.773 --> 00:05:31.086
Let's click Run and examine the order.

00:05:37.397 --> 00:05:40.388
Group one has two consecutive kit labels, then

00:05:40.388 --> 00:05:43.249
group two starts again at kit one, and group

00:05:43.249 --> 00:05:44.549
three does the same.

00:05:44.549 --> 00:05:47.150
The report contains six different pairs.

00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:50.270
The accumulator reaches six because it increases

00:05:50.270 --> 00:05:53.261
after every label, including labels from later

00:05:53.261 --> 00:05:56.122
groups; it never resets at a group boundary.

00:05:56.222 --> 00:05:59.132
At the end of each group, kit advances to three

00:05:59.132 --> 00:06:01.051
and fails its test against two.

00:06:01.051 --> 00:06:03.590
The outer update then advances the group.

00:06:03.590 --> 00:06:06.314
After group three is complete, group becomes

00:06:06.314 --> 00:06:08.048
four and fails its own test.

00:06:08.048 --> 00:06:10.091
Only then does the summary print.

00:06:10.091 --> 00:06:12.692
Both stopping checks matter: the inner one

00:06:12.692 --> 00:06:15.664
finishes a group, and the outer one finishes the

00:06:15.664 --> 00:06:16.097
report.

00:06:16.268 --> 00:06:19.209
We have a working report for several groups.

00:06:19.209 --> 00:06:22.351
The actual request has two groups with two kits

00:06:22.351 --> 00:06:25.225
each, so we'll change only the outer loop's

00:06:25.225 --> 00:06:26.027
upper limit.

00:06:26.027 --> 00:06:28.968
The inner rule, the label statement, and the

00:06:28.968 --> 00:06:31.575
accumulator will stay in their existing

00:06:31.575 --> 00:06:32.243
positions.

00:06:38.494 --> 00:06:40.543
The new group limit is in place.

00:06:40.543 --> 00:06:43.553
Before running this version, write every output

00:06:43.553 --> 00:06:45.922
line in order, including the summary.

00:06:45.922 --> 00:06:48.868
Trace one complete inner loop before advancing

00:06:48.868 --> 00:06:51.878
the group, and remember when the count changes.

00:06:51.878 --> 00:06:54.119
Pause here if you'd like more time.

00:07:02.184 --> 00:07:05.392
Let's click Run to compare your predicted pairs

00:07:05.392 --> 00:07:08.259
and total with the actual workshop report.

00:07:13.047 --> 00:07:16.033
The first two lines belong to group one, and the

00:07:16.033 --> 00:07:17.838
next two belong to group two.

00:07:17.838 --> 00:07:20.824
Each group starts with kit one and ends with kit

00:07:20.824 --> 00:07:21.073
two.

00:07:21.073 --> 00:07:23.064
The summary reports four labels.

00:07:23.064 --> 00:07:25.988
Check both the pair order and the total against

00:07:25.988 --> 00:07:28.912
your prediction: the right total alone wouldn't

00:07:28.912 --> 00:07:31.836
prove that every required pair appeared exactly

00:07:31.836 --> 00:07:32.147
once.

00:07:32.299 --> 00:07:34.696
We've built the assistant's complete label

00:07:34.696 --> 00:07:35.096
report.

00:07:35.096 --> 00:07:37.778
Each for loop provides a clear start, test, and

00:07:37.778 --> 00:07:40.289
update; nesting makes the inner kit sequence

00:07:40.289 --> 00:07:42.686
repeat for each group; and the accumulator

00:07:42.686 --> 00:07:44.684
counts the labels actually printed.

00:07:44.684 --> 00:07:47.366
Keeping that count outside both loops preserves

00:07:47.366 --> 00:07:50.106
the work from every group, while the final false

00:07:50.106 --> 00:07:52.674
tests stop the report at its intended limits.

00:07:53.476 --> 00:07:56.720
For your next experiment, imagine the inner loop

00:07:56.720 --> 00:07:59.895
starts at kit two instead of kit one, with both

00:07:59.895 --> 00:08:01.450
upper limits unchanged.

00:08:01.450 --> 00:08:04.558
Which required labels would disappear, and how

00:08:04.558 --> 00:08:06.180
would the count respond?

00:08:06.180 --> 00:08:09.153
Explain the missing work before you run that

00:08:09.153 --> 00:08:09.626
change.
